In this lesson we'll look at how to solve trig equations that have been stretched, shifted, and squished around. Don't worry, the recipe is exactly the same whether you're dealing with sine, cosine, or tangent. First, you isolate the trig function itself, meaning you get rid of any constants added on or multiplied through, so the trig part is alone on one side. Then you hit both sides with the inverse function, like arcsine or arccosine or arctangent, to undo the trig. Next, you undo any horizontal scaling by dividing through by whatever number is sitting in front of the x inside the brackets. And finally, you just solve for x. Now here's the catch. Your calculator only spits out one answer, because the inverse trig functions are locked into a restricted range. So you have to use the periodicity of the original function to find every other solution on the circle. And if the number you're feeding in is a special angle, you'll get an exact answer straight off the trig table. Now let's see it in action.